__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
receipts/*
!receipts/README.md
# MEASURED TRAP, repaired. `receipts/*` ignores every evidence receipt, so
# `git add -A` would carry ZERO of them while a report could still claim
# "receipts committed". Phase 8/9 receipts are named explicitly. Scratch
# files (`_*.txt`) stay ignored on purpose: they are console dumps, not
# evidence, and are deleted rather than committed.
!receipts/phase*.json
!receipts/kernel_*.json
# PHASE 10: the reproducible file identity lives in these sidecars, because a
# file cannot contain its own SHA256. Ignoring them would make every receipt
# unverifiable in a fresh clone: the embedded field is the pre-insertion hash
# and is NOT reproducible by design. `sha256sum -c` on the sidecar is the
# check a reader can actually run.
#
# MEASURED TRAP, avoided. A bare `!receipts/*.json.sha256` un-ignores ALL
# sidecars, including 11 whose `.json` is itself untracked (`margin_law`,
# `ingress_smoke_full_D`, `stage8_readout_full_D`, `tau_veto_*`,
# `readout_*`, `nonunit_*`, `learned_codebook_law_full_D`). A fresh clone
# would then hold sidecars whose targets are absent and `sha256sum -c`
# would fail. That is the same defect one level up. The negation is
# narrowed to the prefixes whose receipts ARE tracked.
!receipts/phase*.json.sha256
!receipts/kernel_*.json.sha256
data/
# Staged benchmark corpora are NEVER committed. MMLU 6.4 MiB, GPQA 1.3 MiB,
# MBPP 0.55 MiB. Every receipt records the sha256 of the file it read, so a
# fresh clone can re-stage and verify it reached the same bytes. Digests are
# recorded in docs/EVIDENCE_REGISTER.md section 12.
